  "full_response": "Software testing is a critical phase in the software development lifecycle aimed at identifying bugs, ensuring quality, and verifying that the software meets the intended requirements. Here are the main types of software testing and recommendations on when each should be used:\n\n1. **Unit Testing**\n   - **Purpose**: Test individual components or units of the software to ensure they function correctly.\n   - **When to Use**: During the development stage, developers typically conduct unit tests after writing functional components or classes. It is part of Test-Driven Development (TDD) in agile methodologies, ensuring the smallest testable parts of the application work as expected before they are integrated.\n\n2. **Integration Testing**\n   - **Purpose**: Verify the interfaces and interaction between integrated units/components.\n   - **When to Use**: Once individual components are tested and work correctly, integration testing is conducted to ensure these components work together as expected. This step usually follows unit testing and precedes system testing in the SDLC.\n\n3. **System Testing**\n   - **Purpose**: Evaluate the complete and integrated software system to ensure it meets specified requirements.\n   - **When to Use**: Conducted after integration testing, system testing involves a comprehensive evaluation of the entire system to check for compliance with both functional and non-functional requirements.\n\n4. **Acceptance Testing**\n   - **Purpose**: Validate the software for acceptability. This is often done to check the system against user or business requirements.\n   - **Types**: \n     - **User Acceptance Testing (UAT)**: Conducted by the end-users or customers to ensure the software can support day-to-day business processes.\n     - **Beta Testing**: An informal type of UAT where software is released to a select group of the public to elicit feedback from real users.\n   - **When to Use**: Performed after system testing but before final deployment, as it gives both the consumers and developers confidence that the system meets the agreed-upon requirements.\n\n5. **Functional Testing**\n   - **Purpose**: Verify that each feature or function of the software operates according to the specified requirements.\n   - **When to Use**: Conducted during the system testing phase, generally after integration testing, to verify specific features and operations against defined requirements.\n\n6. **Non-Functional Testing**\n   - **Purpose**: Evaluate non-functional aspects of the software, such as performance, usability, reliability, and security.\n   - **Types**:\n     - **Performance Testing**: Checks the speed and responsiveness of the application under a given workload.\n     - **Security Testing**:",
